Add a --tsv flag to the CLI so it reads tab-separated input. Keep the change small.

Context: bin/csv2json.js reads all of stdin and prints `JSON.stringify(parse(text))` plus a newline. It ignores every argument. src/parse.js already has `parse(text, sep = ',')`, which splits rows on \r?\n and fields on sep. test/csv.test.js uses node:test and runs the CLI with execFileSync. README.md shows only CSV usage. `npm test` runs `node --test`.

Steps:
1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether `process.argv.slice(2)` includes '--tsv'. The flag may appear anywhere among the arguments. If it is there, call `parse(text, '\t')`. If not, call `parse(text)` exactly as today. Input still comes only from stdin. Keep the output format exactly as it is: compact JSON and one trailing newline. Leave unknown arguments alone: don't add --help, a --sep/--delimiter option, auto-detection or quoting rules. src/parse.js should not need changes.
2. Add tests to test/csv.test.js using node:test and execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' }). Assert the exact strings:
   - input 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]\n'
   - input 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' -> '[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]\n'
   - input 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]\n'
   Also add a test that runs without the flag on 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' and expects the output to equal JSON.stringify([{ 'a\tb': '1\t2' }]) + '\n'. Keep the existing tests.
3. In README.md, add a section that documents --tsv. Include the example `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and say that the flag means tab-separated input, with fields split on a single tab. Keep the CSV example.
4. From the repo root, check that each of these prints exactly what is shown:
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` -> [{"a":"1","b":"2"}] and exit 0
   - `printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` -> [{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` -> [{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]
   - `printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` -> [{"a":"1","b":"2"}]
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` -> [{"a\tb":"1\t2"}], where \t is the JSON escape
   Then run `npm test`, which must exit 0.
5. Commit and open one pull request that covers all of this.